<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=hot-air-balloon">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hot air balloon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1783">1783</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A hot air balloon is a lighter-than-air aircraft whose envelope holds heated air, with a basket or gondola for passengers and a source of heat hung beneath it. The French brothers Joseph-Michel and Jacques-Étienne Montgolfier developed one at Annonay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardeche">Ardèche</a> and flew it unmanned in public in September 1783. On 21 November 1783 Jean-François Pilâtre de Rozier and François Laurent d'Arlandes made the first untethered manned flight, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in a Montgolfier balloon. The French used a balloon for observation at the battle of Fleurus in 1794, and Ed Yost and Jim Winker developed modern balloons with an onboard heat source from the 1950s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hot-air-balloon.jpg" alt="Hot air balloon" width="330" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kropsoq · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2006_Ojiya_balloon_festival_011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1783</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Annonay, Ardèche, France</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Joseph-Michel Montgolfier · Jacques-Étienne Montgolfier</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Sky lanterns, used for military signals by Zhuge Liang in the Three Kingdoms era, were a precursor; in 1709 Bartolomeu de Gusmão showed an unmanned demonstration of his Passarola to John V of Portugal at the Casa da Índia.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=hot-air-balloon">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
